simple question what's actually healthy  for you to eat we know this is good for  you what is this what about this this or  this if you're confused over what is and  isn't healthy to eat you're not alone  knowing what to eat is complicated and a  big reason it's so complicated  is as green as your leafy vegetables so  here's the situation a draft of the most  recent US government dietary  recommendations said that a healthy diet  is lower in red and processed meats  which have been associated with  increased death rates from cancer and  heart disease that's until the meat  lobby caught wind of it now the caution  was dropped from the guidelines in  addition to the food lobbies the  relative youth and unreliability of  nutrition science just adds to the  confusion fats are a perfect example in  the 80s scientists associated the  saturated fats and butter and cream with  heart disease causing consumers to  switch to margarine shortly when  scientists later concluded the trans  fats in those alternatives were more  dangerous people started avoiding fats  altogether resulting in the low and  nonfat dietary trend in the 90s that led  many to increase their carbohydrates and  sugars which experts say helped cause  the obesity and type 2 diabetes epidemic  in America still the connection between  dietary fats and disease is far from  settled  that's because nutrition science relies  on observational studies which can't  definitively connect the cause and  effect so for example all of these come  from observational studies the problem  is it's impossible to know for sure if  these connections are real because so  many factors can affect the outcome now  here's the argument the 2016 nutritional  guidelines won praise for being very  specific about what you should be eating  like vegetables and whole grains but  they were criticized for keeping the  what not to eat section a vague list of  nutrients for example instead of saying  avoid soda they say to avoid excess  sugar once again critics say food  lobbyists are to blame  at 204 pages many say the guidelines on  what to eat are simply too long too  complicated and too influenced by  outside forces some nutrition experts  think the US should just keep it simple  eat in moderation lots of fruit and  vegetables and go easy on the salt and  sugar
 

